\n\n What fresh nonsense is this? Climate \u2018reparations\u2019 are coming, to compensate other countries for the \u2018extreme weather\u2019 they\u2019ve been experiencing, which is allegedly our fault, because a model which has always been wrong said so. The U.K. has now backed the transparent scam. The Telegraph has more. Britain has opened the door to paying climate change reparations to developing countries by supporting talks on the issue at the COP27 summit. On Sunday, at the meeting in Egypt, U.K. negotiators backed a last-minute agreement to address \u201closs and damage\u201d payments to countries badly affected by climate-related disasters. Rishi Sunak will appear at the conference in Sharm el-Sheikh on Monday, where he will pledge \u00a365.5 million for green technology in developing countries. The Prime Minister will say: \u201cBy honouring the pledges we made in Glasgow, we can turn our struggle against climate change into a global mission for new jobs and clean growth. And we can bequeath our children a greener planet and a more prosperous future. That\u2019s a legacy we could be proud of.\u201d\n\nBoris Johnson will also deliver a speech warning against the \u201cnaysayers\u201d who threaten Net Zero targets. Pakistan is leading a push by developing countries including Bangladesh and the Maldives for compensation from richer countries responsible for most of the world\u2019s pollution. The U.K. backed the issue being on the COP27 agenda during two days of negotiations ahead of the Egypt summit and is understood to accept that a deal must be done over the economic cost of climate change, which is forecast to reach $1 trillion by 2050. On Sunday night, a Downing Street source said Mr Sunak planned to \u201cscale up progress and support\u201d for developing countries suffering the worst effects of global warming. The Prime Minister will come under pressure in Egypt to agree to a costly deal on reparations at the same time as he draws up plans for sweeping domestic tax rises and spending cuts. On Sunday night, Treasury sources said Jeremy Hunt had signed off up to \u00a335 billion in spending cuts and up to \u00a325 billion in tax hikes to fill an estimated financial black hole of \u00a360 billion. Labour has backed calls for the U.K. to pay other countries affected by climate change, with Ed Miliband, the shadow climate minister, calling it a \u201cmoral responsibility\u201d.", "language": "en", "metadata": {"sentence_id": "1"}, "frames": [{"name": "Cardinal_numbers", "roles": [{"name": "Number", "filler": "Trillion", "idxs": [4, 4]}, {"name": "Multiplier", "filler": "1", "idxs": [3, 3]}], "idxs": [4, 4], "activation": "Trillion"}, {"name": "Age", "roles": [{"name": "Entity", "filler": "nonsense", "idxs": [13, 13]}, {"name": "Age", "filler": "fresh", "idxs": [12, 12]}], "idxs": [12, 12], "activation": "fresh"}, {"name": "Rejuvenation", "roles": [{"name": "Entity", "filler": "Climate \u2018", "idxs": [5, 6]}], "idxs": [7, 7], "activation": "Reparations"}, {"name": "Causation", "roles": [{"name": "Effect", "filler": "Climate \u2018 reparations \u2019 are coming , to compensate other countries for the \u2018 extreme weather \u2019 they \u2019ve been experiencing , which is allegedly our fault", "idxs": [17, 43]}, {"name": "Cause", "filler": "a model which has always been wrong said so", "idxs": [46, 54]}], "idxs": [45, 45], "activation": "because"}, {"name": "Judgment", "roles": [{"name": "Reason", "filler": "which", "idxs": [39, 39]}, {"name": "Evaluee", "filler": "our", "idxs": [42, 42]}], "idxs": [43, 43], "activation": "fault"}, {"name": "Exemplar", "roles": [{"name": "Type", "filler": "model", "idxs": [47, 47]}], "idxs": [47, 47], "activation": "model"}, {"name": "Statement", "roles": [{"name": "Medium", "filler": "a model which has always been wrong", "idxs": [46, 52]}, {"name": "Message", "filler": "so", "idxs": [54, 54]}], "idxs": [53, 53], "activation": "said"}, {"name": "Weather", "roles": [{"name": "Spec